WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
This hanging mass is made entirely of bees.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.120
The swarm can hold itself together

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.840
even when shaken about by the wind.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.120
Now a team of scientists have been finding out how.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:19.180
About once a year, colonies of honeybees leave their nests in swarms

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.800
searching for a new home.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.880
Until they find a new site the bees rest

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.200
in these temporary clusters.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.820
The clusters are often found in trees

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.520
but this one is forming in a lab

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.580
at Harvard in Massachusetts.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.400
little do the bees know they're about to go for a ride.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.620
The researchers built this shaking apparatus

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.260
to investigate how the bee swarm reacts to movement.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.640
When shaken from side to side,

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.240
shearing forces act on the colony,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.560
with the bees at the top taking the brunt of it,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.200
as they try to hold up everything beneath them.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.540
The forces threaten to destabilize the whole colony.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.100
So the bees do something clever

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.720
they spread out.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.780
The flatter shape changes the colony's centre of mass,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.940
and so it wobbles less

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.520
reducing the pressure on the bees.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:17.540
It makes sense,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.380
imagine you're standing up and you
find the ground shaking beneath you

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.200
instinctively you'd crouch down, same principle.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.780
Anyway, what's particularly interesting here

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.980
is that the forces at play are
acting on the swarm as a whole,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.680
but each bee can't see the shape of the swarm -

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.640
so how does it know where to go or what to do?

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.300
To find out researchers tracked
the bees on the outside of the swarm.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.060
As the colony shook they moved upwards

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.680
to where the stress is greatest.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.500
The researchers modelled this behaviour

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.420
and found that if all the bees follow the rule

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.940
- move towards the area of higher stress -

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.660
the colony flattens out,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.480
increasing its stability

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.280
and so decreasing the average stress on each bee.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.536
Great for the colony

00:02:02.536 --> 00:02:07.560
but individual bees are actively putting 
themselves under more stress to do it.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.020
This kind of altruistic behaviour

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.340
is not very common in nature

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.780
except, that is, in eusocial insects like bees and ants

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.060
which rely on altruism for survival.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.100
What's more

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.700
even though each individual bee
doesn't necessarily know what's going on,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.740
by simply moving towards an area of higher stress

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.500
the colony as a whole acts intelligently.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.060
This is called emergent intelligence

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.980
and some scientists think that
it's through a process like this

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.340
that individual neurons firing in our brain

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.540
can collectively come together to make, well

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.780
us.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.500
Talk about a hive mind

